5:What makes a great adventurer dance for joy in the streets, his eyes gleaming with excitement and hollering like a child?
6:A movie.
9:If they knew that "Fortunes of Adventure" was the movie that guided this man onto the path of adventure, I think most people would not be surprised.
10:Now, this decades-old movie is getting a re-release in the cinema. Die-hard fan Joseph had bought a whole book of tickets, and was using every opportunity—including when playing Mahjong—to rave about it. I happened to be free the day of the first screening, so went along with him.
12:The re-release naturally makes old fans happy, but what really makes Joseph ecstatic was the news announced by the producer at the end of the screening...
17:It took over half an hour for Joseph's excitement to dissipate, or at least calm down to a manageable level. While he rests against a wall, I go to pick up some drinks from a nearby vending machine.
25:Hmm... I don't have much planned for tomorrow, so might as well make the most of this chance. After all, an all-night screening should be fun.
32:I feel so dumb after learning that the so-called "foreshadowing" was just a blooper, for I was really pondering when watching "Fortunes of Adventure 2"...
37:Joseph gets up and grabs an old-looking videotape from the shelf.
44:He fast-forwards, before pausing on a scene where the protagonist is surrounded by a group of sailors. Smiling, he points at someone in the corner.
45:{character:player} I... Where have I seen this face before?
46:I look carefully at the man on the screen, then turn to face Joseph.
47:As he intentionally pulls the same face as the man, I'm sure I'm on the right track.
54:Joseph goes into the kitchen and opens the fridge, but closes it again after taking one look. He turns around, scratching his neck a little awkwardly.
62:Did I say something weird? Even George, who has been eating quietly beside me, tilts his head to look at me.
69:Joseph pulls up a photo on his phone. It shows a room, piled high with all manner of strange trinkets.
76:...I think this is the first time I've ever seen Joseph so serious.
79:George spreads his wings and pats me on the shoulder, then closes his eyes and shakes his head. His little noises seem to say "Chill out, man. It's not this guy's first rodeo."
81:It's not a question of luck... There's a lot of valuable stuff in there.
92:Watching Joseph gently swipe the screen of his phone, it is like I can peer through time. I can perfectly imagine him carefully wiping those "trophies" in the past.
103:...I rack my brain, but can't for the life of me understand what Joseph means by his strange remark.
110:It's hard for people to visualize things they've never seen before, and I certainly can't imagine what this overwhelmingly positive and optimistic guy was like when depressed.
120:That afternoon, the two discussed adventures and travel while they ate. Although Joseph hadn't gone out for a while, and had only old stories to tell, it was still an eye-opening experience for his friend.
121:As they talked, Joseph pulled out a curiously-patterned ornament. His friend played around with it for a while, and seemed unable to put it down. Seeing that he couldn't tear himself away from it, Joseph simply gave it to him to keep.
122:His largess caused his friend to tease him, that he didn't care enough about his treasures to give them away so casually. But Joseph told him to treat it as an early birthday present, laughingly saying "Not so casual now, is it?"
123:Watching his friend leave, humming happily, he thought the little ornament looked even better hanging from his friend's waist.
125:The last bit of Joseph's reluctance disappeared along with his friend. "There's nothing to regret. Who's to say there won't be something better, just around the corner?" Thinking this, his entire being somehow felt lighter.
128:He suddenly recalled the words of a somewhat dissolute gentleman in a book he had once read.
129:"People only think of possessing, little realizing they are possessed."
130:While Joseph didn't appreciate this gentleman's cynical attitude, this was one saying which did somewhat resonate with him now.
131:"Memories hinder the future, and allow the past to invade."
134:And so it was that, after a sleepless night, Joseph began to go out with his treasures.
135:Some he gave to friends, while some he left in the street market for others to discover.
137:Gradually, more and more space was cleared in his home, until it was practically empty.
140:He was no longer trapped by nostalgia, by all the "memories" attached to things, and his old haunts took on a different look in his eyes.
141:As for the new treasures? He gave most of them away to people he meet as a token of their friendship after sharing the adventure stories related to the treasures...
147:After hearing such profoundly heartfelt words, I feel like now I really know this man in front of me...
150:I still have many thoughts fluttering around my mind, but right now I can't think of a better word for him than "awesome".
154:Joseph... My super adventurer.
158:Actually, I think Joseph really is embarrassed. After manically praising him so emotionally, even I'm now embarrassed for gushing so much myself, to say nothing of how he must feel! It's just his personality makes it almost easy for him to admit this.
159:Luckily, Joseph set an excellent example as to how to avoid awkwardness: confidence! So long as you come across confidently, and speak at a decent volume, then there's no need to let embarrassment make an awkward atmosphere!
170:Am I overthinking things? I always get the feeling that George is looking down on us with utter disdain... But maybe I should keep it down, no matter how excited I am. It's late, after all, and I don't want to disturb the neighbors.
172:The arrival of the delivery guy certainly calms our friendly discussion. Stocked up with drinks and snacks, we press play once more on our viewing party...
176:I never made it to the end... I don't know when I dozed off, but the next thing I know I open my eyes and see the light outside...
184:...Got away with it.
185:Adventurers really are observant sorts. I stay silent for only a second, but he senses that something is off.
186:I lied. I was silent just now because what Joseph said actually made me a little uneasy. His attitude to exploring, and the sacrifices he's made to make progress... His kind of unrestrained manner is certainly impressive.
187:It's precisely because of his ability to decisively say goodbye to the past that I can't help but worry. I wonder if our memories together, as well as the other relationships he's formed in One Han City, might also one day become an impediment to his travels.
188:If this really is the case... How will Joseph deal with this bit of "the past"?
189:With drinks and snacks restocked, we press play on our viewing party once again. But I can't concentrate on the movie anymore, and that question is the only thing in my mind.
190:I can't think of an answer. Or maybe I should say... I dare not dwell on it.
191:And it's not like I can ask Joseph this directly...
192:I didn't see Joseph for a long time after our viewing party. So long, in fact, that people began to miss his hearty laughter at Mahjong tables.
193:As a busy adventurer, it wasn't strange that he wouldn't see anyone, being out and about from dawn to dusk. But every time I think back to our conversation that night, I can't help but feel a little uneasy.
194:I'm so anxious that I gradually start feeling as if he was never really here. This feeling grew only stronger after the discussion at the Mahjong table...
207:I have only myself to blame for not paying enough attention. I check, it's Riichi Ippatsu, and I just helped her get a high score with Pure Double Sequence...
208:{character:Joseph} ...
208:{character:George} ...
208:{character:player} ...
209:Are you leaving One Han City, Joseph?
212:I have only just spoken before I dealt into another Haneman, and bring the round to an early conclusion.
213:Months pass in the blink of an eye. The weather grows cold, with snowstorms practically every week this entire month.
214:This whole time, I still haven't seen Joseph. Maybe Kana was right, and he just slipped away from One Han City.
215:In pursuit of progress, maybe it's his old trick of nonchalantly bidding farewell to the "past"...
218:Today is Christmas Eve. This time last year, unbeknownst to us, Joseph had dressed up as Santa, and jumped from a helicopter to land at the shrine. It was a pretty great surprise.
219:Ichihime seems to have really been looking forward to it this year. So as her provider, it's fallen to me to play the part of Santa this year! I've prepared for this for many days, and spent so much money that it blew a hole in my wallet, but as long as people have fun, it'll be worth it.
220:That's what you thought too, right Joseph?
221:To be honest, I'm not as passionate as he was, and only wanted to do this due to some personal reasons. I think that by doing this, I can cheat myself... Give myself the illusion that he hasn't totally gone...
224:...Enough! No more thinking about Joseph. Gotta get my smile on! It's not right for Santa to be scowling!
226:Eh... Th-this laughter can't be...?!
227:I look back, only to see the Santa who just brushed past me remove his beard, and reveal a hearty, familiar smile.
229:I hesitate for a long while, before walking up to him, stretching out my hand, and gently pinching his face along the curve of his smiling lips.
240:I tell Joseph what happened that day at the Mahjong table, and he nods with a wry smile.
247:If anyone else had said that to me, I would have thought they were full of it. But it doesn't surprise me that such a thing could really happen to this guy.
261:Joseph stops walking and turns his head as he lights a cigarette. Walking in the direction he is now looking would, if I remember correctly, bring us to the One Han City port.
265:Language truly is power. With just a few words, it's possible to calm stormy emotions that have seethed for months... Actually, maybe it's better to say that it's just this man's strength. His obvious sincerity is enough to make anyone feel at ease.
270:The delicious aroma of cooking reaches us as we approach the shrine, accompanied by the intermittent sounds of laughter.
284:I should have said this to him a long time ago, but had just never found quite the right moment.
285:As the one who had seen him first, I feel I have the right to also be the first in telling him these obvious words...
288:{character:Joseph} Cool! Know what, friend? I am SO PUMPED right now!
289:{character:player} Come back to earth, Joseph, before you float away to space.
291:{character:Joseph} Sorry if I'm a little overexcited, but I just can't help it right now!
292:{character:Joseph} Unbelievable! How many years has it been? And there's gonna be a new "Fortunes of Adventure"?!
293:{character:player} Anything nostalgic is powerful. Be as excited as you like!
294:{character:Joseph} OOOHH YEAHHH!
295:{character:player} Don't forget to stay hydrated, your throat must be parched after shouting so much.
296:{character:Joseph} Thanks... Glug... Glug... Friend. Perfect timing with this water!
298:{character:Joseph} When it's finally released, let's go to the premiere together! I promise I'll get the best seats in the house.
299:{character:player} Sure. I don't know if the new story is a direct sequel to the others. So I'll have to binge them first.
300:{character:Joseph} Well, why not seize the day, as they say? I happen to have the complete Collector's Edition... How about picking up some snacks and coming to binge at mine?
301:{character:player} At yours? I'll think about it...
302:{character:player} Alright! Anyway, I swear I saw something at the end there which struck me. Wasn't that the lost key hanging from the protagonist's waist?
303:{character:Joseph} You're very observant, my friend. Why don't you have a guess at what will happen? And in the meantime, let's go get some snacks!
304:{character:player} Okay. Should we get something for George, too?
305:{character:Joseph} Good idea. He'll get a bit sulky if it's just us stuffing our faces!
306:{character:player} ...So, it's only because the actor forgot to take the key off?
307:{character:player} Why couldn't you have told me this earlier...?
308:{character:Joseph} You'll concentrate more if you have to think while watching a movie, right? Anyway, what did you think? Isn't "Fortunes of Adventure 2" epic?
309:{character:player} Yeah, it's great. But I've started wondering if our friendship is just as epic.
310:{character:Joseph} Hahaha! I'm sorry—forgive my little joke, my friend. By way of apology, let me tell you a little-known easter egg.
312:{character:player} Yeah, you've said before that the "Fortunes of Adventure 1" videotape was your inspiration for adventure.
313:{character:Joseph} I really thought it would just be some old family home videos, but it wasn't. And why was it titled "Youth"? It's really confusing.
315:{character:player} That's reasonable, but your tone tells me that there's more to the story.
316:{character:Joseph} Yes! So, I rewatched it one day when I didn't have a lot to do, and I think I've found the answer... Does this background actor look familiar to you?
317:{character:player} That's your grandfather, right? I get it now! The "Youth" refers to when he was an extra in the movie.
318:{character:Joseph} Bingo! Congratulations on discovering one of the least-known easter eggs in the world!
319:{character:player} Well, it's definitely an easter egg for people who know you... And speaking of that, it's understandable why your grandfather would have kept it so carefully under his bed. It must have been a wonderful memory for him.
321:{character:player} ...The old man had... Quite the sense of humor.
322:{character:Joseph} Hahaha! Thanks for the compliment—humor is a tradition in our family! Oh, it seems we've finished all the drinks we bought. Let me go see what's in the fridge.
323:{character:Joseph} This is awkward... It seems there's not a lot in the fridge. If you give me a minute, I can order something to be delivered.
324:{character:player} Well, you got the snacks, so I should get the drinks.
325:{character:Joseph} No no no! You're my guest, and I must do my best to be an excellent host. You just sit right there and make yourself at home, my friend.
326:{character:Joseph} It's my fault that I wasn't on top of what I had. I've been on location filming for so long, I completely forget these sorts of things.
327:{character:player} Well, you're only here a while, so it's not that surprising.
328:{character:Joseph} Only here a while?
329:{character:George} Screech?
330:{character:Joseph} That's an odd thing to say. I've always lived here.
331:{character:player} Really...? Well, have you considered moving house recently?
332:{character:Joseph} It's great here! What makes you ask that?
333:{character:player} Uh, seems like I misunderstood. I saw that there's only the most basic of daily necessities here, and figured you must have either just moved in or are in the process of moving out.
334:{character:player} On the way here I tried to imagine what your place was like. Now, I think it's... What's the best way to put this... A little too clean. Not quite what I thought an adventurer's place would be.
335:{character:Joseph} Ah, I see. Is this more like what you had in mind, friend?
336:{character:player} Yes! That's it! Just like that!
337:{character:Joseph} Ahaha! Most people expect an adventurer to live in this sort of room. This is my room back in my hometown.
338:{character:player} Wow! It's just like a little museum! When can I go check it out? And, uh, do you charge an entrance fee?
339:{character:Joseph} Oh... I'm sorry to disappoint you, my friend, but I fear that most of what you see here has been given away.
340:{character:player} Hey, you gotta work on your jokes, that was terrible. No one's going to believe that, eh?!
341:{character:player} No way! You really gave them away? Why?!
342:{character:George} Screeeech—!
343:{character:Joseph} What rotten luck! If only we'd met earlier, I could have taken you to look around.
344:{character:player} And not just one or two items, either, but most of what's in this picture? What should I say...
