354:{character:player} Well, that does sound like what you would do.
355:{character:Joseph} That's true. I guess I'm the only one who could be so stupid as to give away the treasures he's worked so hard to collect.
356:{character:player} Come on, don't twist my words. While you're certainly a bit rash, I don't think you're stupid. In fact, I admire your generosity.
357:{character:player} I noticed it while playing Mahjong too. You're loathe to take tiles that you know other people want, and will give them what they need too. I suspect it's the same with all this stuff. If other people show an interest, you'll do your best to make them happy.
358:{character:Joseph} Hahaha! You really get me! Spot on, my friend. As long as someone I like is willing and able to look after and cherish an item, they can take whatever they want.
359:{character:player} Never mind the value... These items were what you've worked so hard to find and bring back. I don't think I could do the same if I were you.
360:{character:Joseph} Certainly. To me, each and every single thing in this photo is priceless. I'd be lying if I said there wasn't a moment's hesitation when giving things away.
361:{character:Joseph} But I've had ample opportunity to enjoy the fruits of my labor. If I can share this joy with others, why not? Besides, by doing this I won't become possessed by these valuable possessions.
362:{character:player} That's really unfortunate.
363:{character:Joseph} Hahah! So you think that as well, eh? Seems like most people feel the same way.
364:{character:Joseph} I consider every single thing in this photo to be a priceless treasure... And yet I gave them all away. Anyone would think I'm a fool.
365:{character:player} Don't talk about yourself like that, that's not true. I, and everyone who knows you, don't think you're stupid.
366:{character:player} I reckon, if you had a friend who really liked a particular object, you'd give it to them—as long as you knew they'd cherish it.
367:{character:Joseph} Oh! So am I quite a generous figure to you, friend?
368:{character:player} You're the same in your Mahjong play, even if there's a slight risk that you'll complete someone's hand. Mahjong reveals one's character.
369:{character:Joseph} Hahaha! You really do get me, my friend. Besides, I've had ample opportunity to enjoy the fruits of my labor. If I can share this joy with others, why not? There's nothing unfortunate about it.
370:{character:player} Well, maybe. But never mind monetary value, you've worked so hard to find every one of these objects. Saying that they are priceless would be an understatement. I imagine saying goodbye time after time to each of these must be tough...
371:{character:Joseph} Tough... Well, I'd be lying if I said there wasn't a moment's hesitation when giving things away.
372:{character:Joseph} But it's good to give things to those who are worthy of being entrusted with them. And anyway, doing this prevents me from becoming possessed by possessions.
373:{character:player} Possessed?
374:{character:player} Being possessed by the treasures you own...? That's a strange thing to say. Surely you possess them, not the other way round?
375:{character:Joseph} Other people think I had an enviable collection of treasures that represented my past glory. But in reality, these treasures were slowly taking over my life. I could not bear to lose them, and could only let them multiply. At the worst, I had so many things that I could barely move around without stepping on something.
376:{character:player} That does sound like a hassle, having to always be mindful of where you walk. Or having to be careful in everything you do, really.
377:{character:Joseph} All that is only the physical aspect, too. The real trouble is the mental. You may find it hard to believe, but there was a brief period when I lost my passion for adventure.
378:{character:Joseph} Part of it was down to sheer physical exhaustion, but mostly it was due to waking up one day and finding the novelty of adventuring had worn off. I didn't know what to do, as everything seemed dull and drab.
379:{character:player} ...That is hard to imagine.
380:Joseph, tired of exploring...! Almost as believable as Jack and the Beanstalk.
381:{character:Joseph} Can't see it, right?! I had thought that nothing could ever make me tire of adventure, but the fact is, I spent almost a full year staying at home, doing nothing...
382:{character:Joseph} I felt completely lost during that time, with no idea what was wrong with me. The only thing I was sure of was that I was ill, and struggling with my mental health.
383:{character:Joseph} I saw a therapist several times, but their assessment of me came back as normal. Apart from losing my joie de vivre, my mood could be considered stable.
384:{character:Joseph} The therapist advised me to stop ruminating, and try to foster some other hobbies. While I could see the reason for this advice, I just didn't want to do it.
385:{character:Joseph} I got the stubborn idea into my head of wanting to identify what caused me to lose interest in adventure. I needed to know the reason, and bid a dignified farewell to the thing I used to love.
387:{character:Joseph} I was racking my brains to no avail, until one day an old friend I hadn't seen in a long time paid me a visit...
388:{character:Joseph} When I chose to give that object away, it was no longer just an ornament, but a symbol of friendship. It looked even more dazzling with that added layer of meaning.
389:{character:Joseph} And then suddenly—and I mean before I could blink—I had an epiphany.
390:{character:Joseph} I turned to look at my private collection of treasures, and found them oppressive. It's not like I suddenly detested them, but I had this incredible realization that my life had somehow been taken over by them.
391:{character:Joseph} I knew one thing for sure... I needed to say a proper goodbye, for all the adventures yet to come.
392:{character:Joseph} Value doesn't even come into it. Giving it away doesn't matter, so as long as it's to the right person. It's like that saying you have here, something about objects bound with people by fate.
393:{character:Joseph} This was definitely a difficult process. I asked myself on many occasions if I was being foolish.
394:{character:Joseph} But finally, as I surveyed the empty room, I felt that familiar fire come roaring over me, and knew it was all worthwhile... Fire, the fire that urged me to adventure, was blazing brightly once more...
395:{character:Joseph} By not allowing myself to have anything, I can avoid comparing the past with the present. Only then is it possible for me to move forward.
396:{character:player} ...You mean, you can't get excited by finding new treasures if you keep the old ones, because you'll have something similar in your collection already?
397:{character:Joseph} That's just it. Treasure-hunting was my motivation for adventuring. I don't want personal issues to cost my passion for finding treasure or respect for nature—to me, that's the height of arrogance. I had to find a way to keep myself hungry, and turn that hunger into action.
398:{character:Joseph} There's no way I can treat everything as truly unique, so can only use the somewhat clumsy "farewell" method. This helps me view the treasures at hand as unique as possible, so that every serendipitous encounter is full of excitement and surprise.
